#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce18d2d3-cbf0-5718-8403-e06f139d53eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce18d2d3-cbf0-5718-8403-e06f139d53eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455260} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6ad64c-d62f-565f-ba15-8bbc501a2f16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186230282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5c1a49a3-d524-53b2-a5dc-a7db56f9b9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343430" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $2$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+5y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+5y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=2.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-5(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 2 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=2{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼2{e}^{-5x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231418 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "742531ae-be2d-5fc8-8ef8-4f745c93bacd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97309906-dff0-599d-b092-0740a0732b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365944} {:answer/id #uuid "833af303-c46f-5b0d-8a79-139d161c6a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "833af303-c46f-5b0d-8a79-139d161c6a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455139} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5699544-5216-55d0-b396-9487609e9ee4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "593a3349-a7fc-5c8f-8d89-90616a43f4a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343757" :question/problem "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=f(x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is a $3$-periodic function. Fill in the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0.$*\n\nThe solution is \n\n$y(x)∼$  \n{{field-1}} \n$+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{c}$ is the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the homogeneous solution. The general solution of ${y}^{′}+4y=0$ is \n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNext, we identify the form of the general solution. Because the operator ${y}^{′}+4y$ involves a first derivative, a periodic forcing function $f(x)$ results in a particular solution containing both sine and cosine terms:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x))\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=1.$ Using the hint that the particular solution part evaluates to zero at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A{e}^{-4(0)}+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 1 & =A(1)+0 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $A$ back into the homogeneous part:\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{e}^{-4x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}x)).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231419 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81f5380b-7a7d-52c5-a530-3768e7cf3143" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Second-Order IVPs Using Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c43daebb-d90e-5f56-8f36-6c2339f67f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365956} {:answer/id #uuid "abb08875-6849-5046-819a-ba8c196e97ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365957} {:answer/id #uuid "3639c708-e27d-5c89-b671-db99d9bc675a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365958} {:answer/id #uuid "730c2a39-24f7-5e69-8ad1-ee3baab0c9b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c43daebb-d90e-5f56-8f36-6c2339f67f42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365956} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab9d6fd-f448-55e9-b96d-b8b72364ed81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdb89387-1831-5dc9-8b54-30b782e40107" :question/math-academy-id "q-343989" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=f(x),\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above, where $f(x)$ is an even, $2\\pi $-periodic function. Select the missing part in the solution of the IVP, where the second infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of a linear differential equation of the form ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(x)$ is given by $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p},$ where ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we address the particular solution ${y}_{p}.$ Since $f(x)$ is an even function and the differential operator contains no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits the symmetry and is also an even function consisting only of cosine terms. This is represented by the term\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNext, we find the homogeneous solution. The characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{2}+8=0$ gives roots $\\lambda =\\pm \\sqrt{8}\\text{i}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we apply the initial conditions. Differentiating the total solution gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)∼-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 0 & =0+B\\sqrt{8}-0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because the total solution is an even function, its derivative is an odd function, which must vanish at $x=0.$\n\nNext, we use the condition $y(0)=-2.$ With $B=0,$ the total solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nApplying $y(0)=-2$ and noting that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =A+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n} \\\\ A & =-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A$ back into ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x),$ we find the missing part:\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)(-2-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n})+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$"}]}
